  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Freddy Fermin and Austin Hays both delivered when it mattered, keeping the Padres relevant through a day game that never got away.",
    "what_this_means": "Randy V\u00e1squez turned in a clean six innings. Three hits, one run, the kind of line that wins games if the lineup executes. The execution arrived in pieces. Fermin went 2-for-4 with three RBIs, which means he didn't just show up; he converted. Hays matched him with two hits and two runs driven in. That's not a breakout offensive day. That's a team that found enough runners to score on and then drove them in when it counted. The seventh inning broke the game open. Three runs crossing after Jake Cronenworth's walk set the table. Fermin and Hays were the ones holding the bat when the chances came. Griffin Canning worked three clean innings after V\u00e1squez exited, which quieted any late noise Arizona tried to make. This is a balanced game: the starter got out without major damage, the pen held, and the lineup didn't waste what it was given. That's the formula when it works.",
    "what_to_watch": "CHC sends a TBD arm tomorrow at 7:00 PM PT. Michael King gets the ball for San Diego, riding three wins in his last four starts into a situation where early efficiency matters. Get through five clean, let the lineup stay close enough to win late.",
